SUMMARY:The Role of the Uniformed Services in India’s Constitutional Democracy
DESCRIPTION:How do the armed forces and the police\, entrusted with the state’s authority\, remain answerable to the Constitution and to the citizens it protects? Two senior former officers\, one from the air force and one from the police\, speak from long experience of service. A discussion with audience interaction follows\, chaired by a human rights advocate. \nIndia’s constitutional democracy rests on institutions that uphold the Constitution\, protect citizens’ rights\, and stay accountable to democratic principles. The uniformed services hold a distinctive place among them\, which makes their institutional responsibilities\, their relationship with democratic governance\, and their part in safeguarding constitutional values worth examining closely. \nThe event is presented in collaboration with The School for Democracy and Asha Dey and family\, in memory of Air Marshal P.K. Dey. \n \nVenue: Seminar Halls\n\n\nThe Seventh P.K. Dey Memorial Lecture\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSd6Quo1msdMTfxKLZJq1xWk3UehT8IX6MwQaSk5PzkWaCex9g/viewform?usp=pp_url)\n*Speakers*: Air Marshal Anil Khosla (Retd.)\, Jacob Punnoose\, Maja Daruwala\n*Event Categories*: Defence & security\, Governance\, In Memory\, Politics\, Society
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/the-role-of-the-uniformed-services-in-indias-constitutional-democracy/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Defence & security,Governance,In Memory,Politics,Society
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Thumbnail_TROTUS.jpg
END:VEVENT
